# Declared content-boundary exemptions, read by scripts/verify-content-boundary.sh.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR
# ---------------------
# `verify-content-boundary.sh` fails when prose that exists in a PRIVATE
# repository of this fleet also exists, verbatim, in a PUBLIC one. Some content
# crosses that line BY DESIGN — design tokens the public design system consumes
# from a private toolkit, governance carriers staged for propagation, a public
# corpus quoted inside a private document. Those are not disclosures and a gate
# that reported them would be switched off within a week.
#
# FORMAT — TAB-separated, exactly three fields, no exceptions:
#
#     <private-path-glob>  <public-path-glob>  <reason>
#
# Both globs are matched against REPOSITORY-QUALIFIED paths, which is how the
# scanner prints them: `design-toolkit/proposed/learning-kit/kit-tokens.css` on
# the private side, `design-system/learning-kit/kit-tokens.css` (umbrella, no
# prefix) or `milosvasic.ru/_layouts/default.html` (submodule, with prefix) on
# the public side. `*` matches any run of characters INCLUDING `/`.
#
# A row with fewer than three non-empty fields is MALFORMED and makes the whole
# run exit 2 — "could not determine" — never 0. A suppression whose scope cannot
# be read is worse than no suppression.
#
# EVERY ROW APPLIES TO ALL THREE KEY SPACES the scanner now builds — the long
# `prose` shingle, the `short` heading/list-item key, and the `name` key. A pair
# is a statement about a FLOW between two paths, and a flow that is legitimate
# for a paragraph is legitimate for a heading drawn from the same file. If that
# is ever not true for some row, split the row rather than widening it.
#
# THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO `name`-ONLY EXEMPTION SYNTAX. Pardoning "this name,
# everywhere" would require writing the name into this file, and this file is
# tracked in a PUBLIC repository — the suppression would be the disclosure. A
# name finding is pardoned the same way as anything else: by naming the two
# paths between which it may travel, and saying why.
#
# WHY PAIRS AND NOT A PUBLIC-PATH DENYLIST. An exemption authorising design
# tokens to reach `design-system/` must NOT also authorise a session transcript
# to reach it. Every row therefore names BOTH sides. Widening either glob widens
# exactly one flow. This is the same discipline `.hardcoded-paths-allow` applies
# ("an exemption always carries its justification inline") and the same reason:
# an unexplained suppression is indistinguishable from a bluff.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* IN THIS FILE
# ---------------------------------------
# The verbatim quotation of `workshop/chapters/01/… Notes by Gemini.PDF` inside
# `docs/workshop-curriculum/RECON.md` is NOT exempted. It is a real finding in a
# public repository and the operator decides what happens to it; silencing it
# here would be the exact failure this instrument exists to prevent.
#
# Editorial overlap between `ai_interviewing/docs/**` and this repository's own
# site copy (`_content/products/**`, `_analysis/content-briefs/**`) is also NOT
# exempted. Those describe public open-source projects and are probably benign,
# but "probably" is not the standard: they are left visible so a human decides
# rather than a glob.
#
# Every row below was derived by RUNNING the scanner and reading what it found,
# not by anticipating what it might find. Re-derive, do not trust undated:
#
#     bash scripts/verify-content-boundary.sh
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The public design system consumes the private toolkit's proposed kit verbatim;
# that transfer is the toolkit's entire stated purpose. Scoped to the kit
# directory on both sides, so toolkit material OUTSIDE `proposed/learning-kit/`
# still fails if it appears in `design-system/`.
design-toolkit/proposed/learning-kit/*	design-system/learning-kit/*	the public design system consumes the private toolkit's proposed learning kit by design; this is a publication path, not a disclosure

# `design-toolkit/qa/` holds the shared token/QA check definitions that
# `_tests/helixqa/` executes. The two are required to agree; that is the point.
design-toolkit/qa/*	_tests/helixqa/*	shared QA check definitions that the umbrella harness executes; divergence would be the defect, not the agreement

# `docs/constitution-adoption/propagation/` exists to hold STAGED COPIES of
# governance carriers on their way into the submodules. Being byte-identical to
# its destination is the function of every file in it, so a match there is the
# mechanism working rather than content escaping.
*	docs/constitution-adoption/propagation/*	staged carrier copies destined for the submodules; byte-identity with the destination is what this directory is for

# The constitution submodule is the UPSTREAM SOURCE of governance text, and it
# is public. `ai_interviewing/docs/**` quotes that public corpus (the matches
# resolve to Constitution.pdf / CLAUDE.pdf / GEMINI.pdf, the constitution's own
# exports), so the flow is public -> private and discloses nothing.
# STATED LIMIT: this row cannot tell that direction apart from the reverse. If
# genuinely private ai_interviewing material were ever pasted INTO the public
# constitution, this row would pardon it. It is scoped to `docs/` for that
# reason and should be narrowed further if that tree stops being quotation.
ai_interviewing/docs/*	submodules/constitution/*	the public constitution corpus is the source; these private docs quote it, so the flow is public-to-private
ai_interviewing/platform/content/*	submodules/constitution/*	interview content generated from the public constitution corpus; same public-to-private direction as the row above

